WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.498 --> 00:00:10.081
(solemn music)

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:15.600
- [Interviewer] In an age of call outs,

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.330
culture wars and perfect facades,

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.980
people can be afraid to
express how they really feel.

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:23.970
So we brought together seven strangers,

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:25.350
protected their identities,

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:28.143
and asked them all seven
burning audience questions.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.253
What will be revealed
when we take the mask off?

00:00:38.673 --> 00:00:41.259
If your answer is, yes,
you'll turn your light on.

00:00:41.259 --> 00:00:42.901
(drum beats)

00:00:42.901 --> 00:00:44.279
(beep) The police.

00:00:44.279 --> 00:00:46.946
(counter beeps)

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:52.257
- [William] I know that there
are quote unquote "good cops"

00:00:52.257 --> 00:00:57.120
but if you are working in the entire force

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.420
with individuals who are
essentially mostly racist

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.500
and where police come
from, the history of it,

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.870
with being the sheriffs of slaves

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:09.420
and tracking down slaves using dogs

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.100
or whatever they're using,

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.930
it's just, wearing that badge,

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:14.400
you're never really here for us,

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.563
and we never feel safe for
that reason so (beep) them.

00:01:17.563 --> 00:01:18.396
(someone chuckles)

00:01:18.396 --> 00:01:20.820
- [Terrence] The only reason
why I can't fully say that,

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:22.980
like he just said there
are some good out there,

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:26.160
and personally having a cousin
who is a police officer,

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:28.020
but everything else behind that

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.060
and all of the other people who introduce,

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:34.110
who bring the racist and pretty
much (beep) mentality to it,

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:36.180
yeah no, it's (beep) them for sure.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:37.020
- [Interviewer] All
right, we're gonna have

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.120
everyone join the conversation.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.020
- [Kennedy] I don't really feel

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.610
anything towards this statement

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:45.930
because I haven't had any
encounters with police, like ever.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:48.000
- [Maurice] Why do you have
to have a personal experience?

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.360
Why can't you just look at the
damage that's has been done-

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.580
- [Kennedy] It's different
'cause I feel like

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.340
I feel the weight of my
ancestors in certain situations,

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.800
in other areas of my life
than I do with the police.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.380
- [William] Okay, this is
not me taking away from

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:02.280
the Black community in general,

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:03.930
'cause police are always targeting us,

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:04.920
but I find it interesting

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:09.060
that only the Black men
had agreed with that.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:09.893
- [Female] Yeah.

00:02:09.893 --> 00:02:11.670
- [Alexus] I'm on the fence with it

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:16.290
because I know that there
are Black cops out there

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:19.590
that are truly trying
to protect and serve,

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.320
and I really don't know what I would do

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:25.710
if we completely destroyed
the police system.

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:27.660
I would be scared because it's like, okay,

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:31.200
well I don't believe that is the solution,

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:35.250
but yes, Black men have that
main encounter with the police.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.020
And sometimes it's
annoying because it's like,

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.300
okay is this the only
cause that y'all care about

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:40.680
is police brutality?

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.620
But yeah, I just, it's halfway with me.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:44.730
- [Terrence] I think feelings do become

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.010
more intense though once
you have an experience.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:47.880
- Yeah, yeah.
- Obviously.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:50.880
- No, definitely.
- And so, seeing situations

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.290
where my dad got pulled over,

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:54.930
got questioned, got
harassed for no reason,

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:57.734
then to experience my
situation down in Texas.

00:02:57.734 --> 00:02:59.340
I mean, I had bought
guns for the first time,

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:02.130
registered them, followed
everything to the T,

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:05.610
and then still get pressed
up against the side of my car

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:06.681
like I did something wrong

00:03:06.681 --> 00:03:08.463
when I did it the exact way
that y'all said I had to do it.

00:03:08.463 --> 00:03:11.970
That experience right there,
my dad's situation started it,

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:14.322
and then my situation
is what finalized it.

00:03:14.322 --> 00:03:15.630
(drum beats)

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:18.225
- [Interviewer] Are
Asians racist towards you?

00:03:18.225 --> 00:03:20.892
(counter beeps)

00:03:22.934 --> 00:03:27.450
- [William] I have a neighbor
who is very privileged.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:29.400
She's married to a white man, she's Asian,

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:33.120
and I feel like that comes
a lot nowadays in America.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:34.980
I feel like Asian women are trying

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.900
to be the white woman of America,

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:38.807
and they're not realizing
that they're also

00:03:38.807 --> 00:03:41.640
in marginalized
communities, just as we are.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:43.800
- [Alexus] Especially Asians that are born

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:45.513
into American culture.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.480
It seems like they're
just pretty much white,

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:53.480
and they don't have any
empathy for other minorities.

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.150
- [Maurice] I have had
negative interactions

00:03:57.150 --> 00:04:01.590
with Asian people or other
people because I'm Black

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:03.300
and because colorism is a thing

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:05.190
but I've also had positive interactions.

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:06.330
So, I feel like, yeah,

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:09.660
Asian is just a very generalized term.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:11.100
- [Jasmin] I know it happens of course,

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:14.160
but me personally I have
not experienced racism

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:15.990
from the Asian community.

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:18.630
I do totally agree with
the idea of colorism

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:21.780
because it does seem
like the darker you are,

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:25.950
the quote "uglier you
are" in any ethnicity,

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:27.660
which does suck.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:30.150
- [Terrence] I think the
American influence is big

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:31.410
and that's on anybody that comes,

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:33.000
no matter where you come
from, what background.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.610
When you get to America, you
spend a couple years here,

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:38.070
you start to see how things are done,

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:40.020
whether they're right or wrong,
you see how things are done,

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.060
how Black people are treated,
how white people are treated.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:43.890
So a little bit after I left the military,

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:46.050
I spent a couple years in Malaysia,

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:49.170
Singapore, Indonesia and South Korea.

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:51.492
These places were places
where I was accepted

00:04:51.492 --> 00:04:53.430
more so than back home.

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:56.610
It was shocking to me to not
be followed around in stores

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:58.020
in South Korea.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:00.180
It was shocking to me to not be treated

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:02.880
or even looked at the same way I would be

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:04.472
walking down the street
in certain places here.

00:05:04.472 --> 00:05:05.820
(drum beats)

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:08.537
- [Interviewer] I have
dated outside my race.

00:05:08.537 --> 00:05:11.204
(counter beeps)

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:15.750
- [Kayla] In my past
dating history I feel like

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:17.319
I tasted the rainbow pretty much.

00:05:17.319 --> 00:05:18.630
(panelists laugh)

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:22.560
I've dated East Asian man,
I've dated Hispanic men.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:24.213
It's different.

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:27.990
You're never gonna find
somebody outside of your race

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:30.210
that's gonna a hundred
percent understand you,

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:32.040
understand your struggle,
understand your family,

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.050
why you do things the way that you do.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.540
When I'm dating them I know
it's gonna be different,

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:40.020
and I feel that's why I'm entertaining

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:42.060
the fact of even doing that.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:42.893
- [Maurice] I don't think that

00:05:42.893 --> 00:05:45.480
I would necessarily date a white person.

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:48.000
I just feel like there's a disconnect,

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:51.180
but as long as they're willing to

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:52.320
have those conversations with me

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.360
and understand my blackness,

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:56.310
I wanna have those
conversations with them,

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:59.190
and I don't mind what their race is.

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:00.023
- [Terrence] I think for me,

00:06:00.023 --> 00:06:02.340
it's just all about my
comfort level with you

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:03.750
and how much I can relate.

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:06.870
And like he said, I think a white woman

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:09.150
would be at the bottom of the totem pole

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:12.780
just because we don't have
that much to connect on,

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:14.763
but I'm not opposed to it.

00:06:14.763 --> 00:06:17.310
I would just kind of
rather work with something

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:20.073
that I know there's a lot
of compatibility there.

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:23.340
- [Kennedy] I'm not opposed
to dating outside my race,

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:24.300
but in terms of marriage,

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:27.090
I would love to marry a Black man, yeah.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:28.290
- [William] Think about our ancestors.

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:30.510
They're probably turning in their grave.

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:31.830
They're like, what are you doing?

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:34.830
We've done so much and you
wanna be with this white person

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:36.720
or this non-Black person?

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:39.330
- [Alexus] I've never
dated outside of my race,

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:40.350
but maybe I should

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:43.852
because a lot of Black men
tend to go to non-Black women.

00:06:43.852 --> 00:06:45.840
- [Male] 'Cause they can control them.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:46.974
- [Alexus] Yes, and because-

00:06:46.974 --> 00:06:48.210
- [Female] 'Cause they're easier.

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:51.240
- [Alexus] And because they
have European features,

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:55.800
softer hair, they like the
Kim Kardashian-looking woman.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:57.810
You know, you want the
Black woman aesthetic

00:06:57.810 --> 00:06:59.580
but you need the European features.

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:01.620
So, maybe I should date
outside of my race,

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:03.480
but I personally, like you said,

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:05.250
I wanna build with a Black man.

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:08.340
I wanna have Black children.

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:09.900
That's what I want to do,

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:12.570
but with this generation of Black men,

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:14.430
I don't know if it's possible.

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:16.380
- [William] Me having sex with a white man

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:18.480
is just, it's the master aspect.

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:20.310
It's the buck breaking history,

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:22.020
if you know what buck breaking is.

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:23.790
It's all of that combined.

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:26.250
And then, the idea of most times,

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:28.260
even in heterosexual relationships,

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:32.970
it's usually a fetish for the
individual to be with you.

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:35.580
It's either because you have a nice body

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:37.500
or your skin is so dark and lovely

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:41.370
or because you have, you know,
that tool that they want,

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:42.694
if y'all know what I'm talking about.

00:07:42.694 --> 00:07:43.602
(panelists laugh)

00:07:43.602 --> 00:07:45.300
So yeah, in general, it's always something

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:46.530
on your body that they want.

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:48.780
And if it's genuinely true
love, that can happen,

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:49.613
but for the most part,

00:07:49.613 --> 00:07:52.061
I just think interracial
dating is disgusting.

00:07:52.061 --> 00:07:53.250
(drum beats)

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:56.645
- [Interviewer] I've felt
oppressed by my own community.

00:07:56.645 --> 00:07:59.312
(counter beeps)

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:04.590
- [Alexus] I actually sometimes
feel very uncomfortable

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:07.290
in spaces full of Black people.

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:10.410
If you're not desirable as a Black woman,

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:12.960
if you're not desirable to men

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:15.360
then you tend to get overlooked.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:19.230
I have to work my ass off
for things that I want.

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:23.250
Black men, CIS hetero
Black men specifically,

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:25.620
they oppress all of us and it sucks

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:27.360
because I wanna be with a Black man

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:31.890
but it's like, I have to try
to appeal to my oppressor.

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:34.620
- [William] I'm gay and I'm also Black.

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:36.000
So, within the Black community,

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.430
I feel like, again, when
we go to these protests,

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:40.920
when we go out to these clubs or parties

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:42.930
and I'm in heterosexual spaces,

00:08:42.930 --> 00:08:46.620
I feel like, oh, it's yeah
he's Black, but he's gay,

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:48.030
so we can't really mess with him.

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:49.590
He can't really be in our space.

00:08:49.590 --> 00:08:52.050
And it's like, how is this
your space that you claimed

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:55.230
over a sexuality and a sexual
preference essentially,

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:57.240
rather than us being the same race?

00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:58.073
- [Maurice] Yeah, of course.

00:08:58.073 --> 00:09:01.320
Like you were saying, if
you're in the LGBTQ community,

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:02.703
it's intersectionality.

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:07.380
People are more cool
with me if I'm passing

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:09.210
and they see me as a CIS Black guy

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:13.080
versus if they know I'm trans.

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:15.570
- [Kennedy] In terms of my experience,

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:16.680
I feel like maybe Black people

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:18.270
were gate keeping opportunities

00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:19.710
or trying to be super stingy

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:22.200
or trying to be the token Black person.

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:23.640
They kind of bully other Black people

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:26.070
to making them be like, you
don't deserve to be in my space.

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:28.630
This is my zone. This is the
area that I'm working in.

00:09:28.630 --> 00:09:30.660
And it's like they're trying
to keep other Black people

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:31.770
from being successful.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.510
I feel like I've seen that a lot,

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:35.813
and I feel like that's
happened me before, too.

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:39.090
- [Kayla] When I was living in Mississippi

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:41.340
I was around predominantly Black people.

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:43.020
I didn't feel any different

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:45.300
but now that I'm back here in California

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:47.893
and I'm around other races,

00:09:47.893 --> 00:09:51.810
well I can see why this
could be considered colorist

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:53.850
or I can see how in this situation

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:56.850
maybe I'm feeling like I'm not as worthy

00:09:56.850 --> 00:09:57.870
as these other people,

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:01.530
but I just couldn't use
the word oppressed really

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:04.532
to describe how my experiences have went.

00:10:04.532 --> 00:10:05.365
(drums beat)

00:10:05.365 --> 00:10:07.654
- [Interviewer] Have you ever
wished you weren't Black?

00:10:07.654 --> 00:10:10.321
(counter beeps)

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:15.630
- [Jasmin] I wanna say, record
straight, I love myself now.

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:18.360
I love being a Black woman,

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:22.740
but for so long because
of also family members

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:26.820
feeding into the whole
idea of you're so pretty

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:28.290
because you're lighter.

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:31.710
For so long as a child,

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:34.080
I associated being Black

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:36.240
as being something that was a bad thing

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:38.700
because I saw how people

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:40.920
that were darker than me were treated.

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:44.040
And I was just like, oh, I
don't wanna be this anymore.

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:46.222
But I mean, with a lot of love

00:10:46.222 --> 00:10:51.222
and compassion and hardcore teaching,

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:54.933
I was like being Black is
honestly the best thing ever.

00:10:57.060 --> 00:10:58.214
- [William] We lit.

00:10:58.214 --> 00:10:59.700
(panelists laugh)

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:03.420
I just feel like we're lit and
I get where that comes from.

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:05.040
That's a part of colorism.

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:06.660
And that happens a lot in our community.

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:08.100
And I just feel like

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:12.510
if we all just accepted
our blackness more,

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:14.520
we would just be great.

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:16.740
- [Maurice] When I was
younger, I moved to a town

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:18.810
that was predominantly white and Hispanic.

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:22.320
I never felt specifically
like, oh I wish I wasn't Black,

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:24.360
but I did look at other people

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:26.880
and say, "I wish I had hair like them.

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:29.220
I wish I looked more like them."

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:31.920
- [Alexus] I love being the race that I am

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:33.840
but having 4C hair

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:37.290
and having darker
knuckles, darker kneecaps,

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:40.050
especially in elementary
school and middle school,

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:42.150
getting through that was really hard.

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:46.620
Now at this age, I'm learning
to accept all parts of me

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:49.050
but I still battle with it every day.

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:50.820
- [Kennedy] There were
times where I maybe wish

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:52.530
that I wasn't Black American,

00:11:52.530 --> 00:11:55.920
that maybe I wish I was an
African woman or Brazilian

00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:57.690
because I feel like Black Americans,

00:11:57.690 --> 00:11:59.370
we kind of were disconnected

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:01.650
from our African roots and our heritage.

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:03.030
And that part kind of hurts

00:12:03.030 --> 00:12:05.670
'cause it's like we don't
really have much to call our own

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:08.730
besides Ebonics or A.A.V.E.,

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:11.940
but Black American cultures
ultimately set the tone

00:12:11.940 --> 00:12:13.950
for a lot of modern day pop culture, too.

00:12:13.950 --> 00:12:16.229
So I'm proud of that part, as well.

00:12:16.229 --> 00:12:17.062
(drum beats)

00:12:17.062 --> 00:12:19.350
- [Interviewer] I would
raise my kids differently

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:21.476
than my parents raised me.

00:12:21.476 --> 00:12:24.143
(counter beeps)

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:27.570
- [William] If you
haven't had an experience

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:28.800
with whether sexual abuse

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:30.780
or some type of violence in your home,

00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:32.430
you are lucky in the Black community

00:12:32.430 --> 00:12:34.350
because it happens to so many of us.

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:36.690
And I feel like with me,
experiencing that growing up,

00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:39.090
I would never beat my child.

00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:40.590
I don't want to have to feel

00:12:40.590 --> 00:12:45.094
like beating my child is a
source of them respecting me.

00:12:45.094 --> 00:12:47.074
I want them to know about mental health

00:12:47.074 --> 00:12:50.010
and take their mental
health actually serious

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:51.120
because we don't take it serious

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:53.250
in the Black community at all.

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:54.840
- [Kayla] I love that you mentioned that

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:56.820
specifically about mental health

00:12:56.820 --> 00:12:59.070
because it's something
you can't talk about

00:12:59.070 --> 00:13:00.240
in our community sometimes.

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:02.100
It's like, well, what's wrong with you?

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:02.970
Are you crazy?

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:04.710
I'm gonna whoop your tail.

00:13:04.710 --> 00:13:06.210
You shouldn't be acting this way.

00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:07.050
I give you everything.

00:13:07.050 --> 00:13:08.880
You have no reason to be depressed.

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:12.570
I don't wanna reciprocate
that unto my children

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:13.620
and I don't want them to feel

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:15.540
like if they're feeling depressed,

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:16.800
if they're feeling overwhelmed

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:18.450
I don't want them to think
they can't come to me

00:13:18.450 --> 00:13:19.620
and talk about it.

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:22.200
My parents, they didn't
have that opportunity

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:24.120
to communicate with their parents

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:24.960
because they were raised

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:27.330
in a completely different generation.

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:32.280
So now, I see my mom
was raising me this way

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:33.900
because that's what she's used to

00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:36.063
but I'm gonna cut it off with my line.

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:39.210
- [Alexus] Our kids are
probably gonna be like,

00:13:39.210 --> 00:13:41.490
yeah I ain't doing that
when I have my kids.

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:43.710
Each generation gets wiser

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:45.180
and they learn something else

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:47.490
or have resources to something else.

00:13:47.490 --> 00:13:49.980
I just wanna always highlight

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:52.080
the good things that my parents did.

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:53.820
I've already acknowledged them

00:13:53.820 --> 00:13:56.490
and addressed the bad things with them,

00:13:56.490 --> 00:13:58.890
but now I can forgive and let it go

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:01.800
and just highlight what they did do right

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:04.145
and kind of mix that with my own.

00:14:04.145 --> 00:14:05.340
(drum beats)

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:08.097
- [Interviewer] I can't
be honest about who I am.

00:14:08.097 --> 00:14:10.764
(counter beeps)

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:13.710
- [Maurice] Being Black,
if I'm in a white space,

00:14:13.710 --> 00:14:17.100
I'm constantly like, how am I coming off?

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:20.970
But a lot of times, for me,
in terms of being trans also,

00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:21.960
like, how am I walking?

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:24.390
How am I talking? How am I being received?

00:14:24.390 --> 00:14:26.670
Do these people, are they speaking at me

00:14:26.670 --> 00:14:29.190
or looking at me like
they're comfortable with me?

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:31.650
And it's just pretty tiring sometimes

00:14:31.650 --> 00:14:32.910
when I'm around other people

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:35.280
and I'm constantly checking myself.

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:38.250
- [Kennedy] Since we're in
the age of cancel culture,

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:39.570
it's just really easy.

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:42.060
I feel like even if you wanna be honest,

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:43.200
it's like sometimes you can't

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:45.000
because you have a fear of getting cancer

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:47.160
or fear of people lashing out on you.

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:48.840
And I feel like in
certain senses like that

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:50.321
I can't be completely honest

00:14:50.321 --> 00:14:52.860
in terms of my views on certain things

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:55.713
just because of how
people are going to react.

00:14:57.330 --> 00:15:02.081
- [William] It takes a lot
for a person to be themselves

00:15:02.081 --> 00:15:04.800
and fully own their identity,

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:07.230
no matter who you are or what you are.

00:15:07.230 --> 00:15:09.781
So living in that, I've
learned to own that.

00:15:09.781 --> 00:15:11.880
I'm always blunt. I'm always me.

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:12.930
What you see is what you get.

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:14.670
If you got a problem, you can leave.

00:15:14.670 --> 00:15:16.290
So, (chuckles) yeah.

00:15:16.290 --> 00:15:19.230
- [Alexus] It's exhausting
to try to defend yourself

00:15:19.230 --> 00:15:20.910
all the time about who you are,

00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:21.900
but at the end of the day

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:26.213
I sleep better at night
knowing that I'm just me.

00:15:26.213 --> 00:15:28.880
(upbeat music)

